  "scorer_sha256": "f088567205683cb3bdd9aec05d625dc2e4d8d54466811e340af52cac639ef2ce",
  "spec": "Track D, mode split of ledger lines 29 to 31 (the same ten contexts and the same block cmp_ctx_adapt): the leave-own-family-out generalist (lofo), the init and the own-table random-slot fine-tune (ctxr) by target mode (rr = ridge residual, yj = Yeo-Johnson, rg = rank-Gauss), no baseline. Asked by cl_fwriter at 05:17Z after line 28 showed, post hoc, that a canonical fine-tune on one table repairs rg in 8 of 10 contexts (+0.39 u) while yj is erratic (three contexts lose 0.6 to 1.7 u in yj alone). Registered while the twenty fine-tunes are running (two of them started, none finished) and before any of their blocks exists, so that the alignment story is tested and not rescued. Named sub-predictions: (1) the generalist beats the init in rg on at least 7 of 10 contexts, with a family-mean gain in rg between +0.15 and +0.45 u; (2) in rr the generalist beats the init on 4 to 7 of 10 with a mean between -0.05 and +0.10 u; (3) in yj the mean gain of the generalist is positive (0 to +0.25 u) but at least one context loses more than 0.5 u in yj, that is, the yj damage of line 28 is not specific to canonical slots or to one table. Reading named in advance: if (1) holds and the generalist beats the init on 6 or fewer contexts in both rr and yj, alignment is counted as \"the rank-Gauss mode only\", and the next training wave needs rank-Gauss episodes and a yj-specific check rather than a general claim. Descriptive: ten contexts, one seed, in-corpus init.",
